  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">5 Year Cooperative Agreement for the Conservation of Mesoamerica&apos;s Last Wild Places</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This is funding year 4 of a 5-year cooperative agreement. The purpose of this project is to secure core habitat and maintain a wildlife corridor across the land bridge of Central America for jaguars and other wide-ranging species such as the Bairds tapir, white-lipped peccary, and macaws. This years proposal will focus on ensuring connectivity across Central Americas Wildlife Corridor. Project activities will focus on the Maya Forest in Guatemala and the Moskitia in Honduras and Nicaragua. These border forests are the largest strongholds for jaguars in the region with estimated populations of 375 and 279 jaguars respectively. They have the highest rates of forest loss and are corridors for trafficking wildlife, timber, drugs, people, arms, and other illicit goods northward. This years funds will be used to: (1)improve governance and strengthen wildlife law enforcement through ground patrols, (2) provide in-situ trainings to managers, rangers, and indigenous authorities on SMART (Spatial Monitoring and Reporting Tool) to carry out intelligence based patrolling strategies, (3) develop a National Jaguar Plan for Guatemala, and (4) scale-up technical services to ranchers in protected area buffer zones that have proven to reduce cattle-jaguar conflict and improve the local economy.</narrative>
  </description>
